#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e57bce-4288-5fe3-aa0a-73628c52743d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278746} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3b843e-b72a-51a2-a995-da57e46e487b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279446} {:answer/id #uuid "81d5aaa0-fd9f-568f-9bb1-e082fcdaa7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279754} {:answer/id #uuid "a999c752-b365-5d3b-960d-66d2a2fbec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280097} {:answer/id #uuid "ced37650-ae67-5a4c-bc4a-bb74c5223c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d5aaa0-fd9f-568f-9bb1-e082fcdaa7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279754} :answer-field/id #uuid "5be271dc-3c10-5641-8030-505366b398d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76775e9c-a3b9-5240-b988-cfc2af339188" :question/math-academy-id "q-162764" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-2),(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2+1)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(2,0),(-2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-3$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 2)$ and its minimum value is $-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85aec1e2-53f5-54ed-8559-44e3da4b0d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121512} {:answer/id #uuid "99d569fc-8fe9-5167-9d86-bcf52cecd477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121513} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e16a3a-d004-5266-9e0c-9d803e28d5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121514} {:answer/id #uuid "023cbcbf-45c4-50e3-8d3e-02f1bd461815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121515} {:answer/id #uuid "51149a6f-1f12-5442-ad22-2b382a3caba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51149a6f-1f12-5442-ad22-2b382a3caba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121516} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ab1c0d2-9899-5cea-b60d-8ed13448eab8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "319e140b-45eb-5dbd-91e1-7f34e0399c73" :question/math-academy-id "q-162795" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y\\,$ on the parabola $y-{x}^{2}=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=y-{x}^{2}=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-2x,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=-2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-1 & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(-1,1).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens upwards, there isn't a maximum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the minimum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-1,1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,1),$ and its minimum value is $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8f830f-22a4-5f79-abbe-9554be0e81d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523344} {:answer/id #uuid "43e96650-8ca0-524b-8e9f-31437aefa7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523394} {:answer/id #uuid "4bee1f98-fa33-5093-87bc-2f00c2477543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523408} {:answer/id #uuid "c43a5edc-f944-5fca-9515-c95ccf41820f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523663} {:answer/id #uuid "c8eb3b1c-5d9a-5384-8da8-9f595ee3a2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8eb3b1c-5d9a-5384-8da8-9f595ee3a2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523674} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc58a902-969b-5c49-b676-fba60e52b489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3c769ab-089f-58c3-aaa9-e70d964ccd5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162798" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=x+y\\,$ on the parabola $y+(x-2)^{2}=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=y+(x-2)^{2}=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2(x-2),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda (x-2) \\\\ 1=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }+2 \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}+2=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+{(\\frac{5}{2}-2)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens downwards, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{9}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c1410e0-a84a-59db-9179-4e96144445b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361250} {:answer/id #uuid "84d79ecf-1f02-5b4a-8561-b6bcc9948bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361429} {:answer/id #uuid "98291461-add9-53ed-afd7-b0c2bd00a5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361514} {:answer/id #uuid "f250f6bc-6c44-51cf-a2c2-612e5c87a588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361917} {:answer/id #uuid "f466069f-33e0-5fe4-9e0d-eb960bf7ce61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84d79ecf-1f02-5b4a-8561-b6bcc9948bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361429} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b70f22b-a5a8-585e-b3dd-fa50d2c97d20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8339a664-cc92-5ae4-a4f3-d6ab19787d80" :question/math-academy-id "q-99027" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=1-x-y\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-1=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,1+\\sqrt{2}\\,$ | $\\,1-\\sqrt{2}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),$ and its maximum value is $1+\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75b438b0-5bc9-5848-8c09-4b9737f5876d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361357} {:answer/id #uuid "a6441485-d04f-586c-8c4c-ff98f01eb432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361581} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1f6919-eef4-5eeb-be4a-38877febdda3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361671} {:answer/id #uuid "e75d0f27-b8bb-5a5c-8905-034c15c6f3c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361870} {:answer/id #uuid "faf4cd0b-f3f9-5c75-a7ea-835686f7d019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faf4cd0b-f3f9-5c75-a7ea-835686f7d019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361959} :answer-field/id #uuid "314fa88d-8f92-5297-95d3-76a7ddb9d25f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47aa66c7-074f-539e-9248-f6b2a1659ce1" :question/math-academy-id "q-99030" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=3x-y+6\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=10\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}3=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=10$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 3,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-3,1),\\,(3,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-3,1)\\,$ | $\\,(3,-1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,16\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(3,-1),$ and its maximum value is $16.$"}]}
